			## ActivityPub Won by Being Boring: A PM's Guide to Why the 'Roadmap' Was a Lie

			Let's take this offline and align on scope for a moment: the fediverse didn't win because ActivityPub was the sexiest protocol in the room. It won because it was the most boring—and in our industry, boring is a feature, not a bug. As someone who has sat through countless stakeholder demos where 'revolutionary' integrations turned into 'we'll circle back on that,' I can tell you that the tech that actually ships is the tech that doesn't require a 40-page migration guide and a prayer. ActivityPub was the default option on the menu, and defaults win sprints.

			Engineers will tell you the real story: this was never about innovation. It was about federation that works like email—ugly, imperfect, and good enough to stop the bikeshedding. In my world, that's called 'de-risking the epic.' We love to roadmap 'game-changing decentralized protocols,' but the roadmap is a lie. What actually happens is we pick the standard that lets us close tickets and get back to fighting over story points. ActivityPub was the least worst choice, and least worst is a release criteria I can defend in a steering committee.

			Meanwhile, the business side kept asking for 'Web3' vibes and 'blockchain synergies,' and we all nodded politely while quietly implementing JSON-LD because that's what the OStatus refugees were doing. Sound familiar? That's the gap between what gets said in the quarterly business review and what gets merged into main. As a PM who lurks on engineering forums, I know the difference. The builders knew ActivityPub was boring—that's why they picked it. It didn't promise a utopia; it promised an interop spec that fit on a napkin and didn't require a PhD in distributed systems.

			So let's give credit where it's due: ActivityPub won because it was the 'good enough' option that everyone could stop arguing about. It's the spiritual cousin of the PM's 'Let's take the conservative doorknob design'—it may not get applause in the kickoff, but it gets to production. If you're still chasing the shiny roadmap, remember: the stakeholders will clap for vaporware, but the users will send you a thank-you note when they can actually log in. And in the end, boring ships.

			Now, if you'll excuse me, I have a Gantt chart to realign—again. But this time, at least I know the outcome: the boring protocol will win, the roadmap will be wrong, and somehow we'll still hit the demo. Can we take this offline? My calendar's open next sprint.
			"""
