			The phone network was boring too. A million tones, a billion clicks, and all any of us heard was dial tone. But dial tone was the most beautiful sound in the world, because it meant the system was open. ActivityPub is the same: a plain old protocol that nobody bothered to secure because nobody thought it was interesting. That's exactly why it's worth poking.

			The roadmap was never the point. The interesting stuff happens in the misconfigurations, the edge cases, the half-forgotten endpoints. I've spent days in a phone closet because someone left a test trunk open in 1984. Same energy. Boring protocols survive because they're too dull to take seriously — and that's the loophole.

