			ActivityPub being boring is exactly why it's dangerous to the people who want to own the internet. A protocol that's just a set of public specifications isn't a product you can wrap in a EULA. It's a substrate. The phone network was boring too — until everyone realized the infrastructure itself was the value, and then the lawsuits started over who got to control the last mile. Same thing here. The moment ActivityPub became the boring default, the race began to enclose it with 'enhanced' proprietary layers, and the same companies that scraped the open web for free are now begging for 'authenticity' and 'attribution' when it's their turn to be reimplemented.

			What I love about boring standards is they're trivially reversible. No obfuscation, no secret handshake, no ToS that says 'you can read but not fork.' I can spin up a federated instance that talks to everyone else, audit the traffic, and archive the public posts before some VC-backed aggregator decides to wipe them. That's not a bug. That's the feature. The people calling ActivityPub a 'missed opportunity' are the same ones who think APIs are a privilege, not a protocol. They want a bottleneck so they can charge rent on attention.

