			user: "schema_drift"
			timestamp: "2026-08-14 22:37"
			body: """
			Every time I hear someone call ActivityPub 'boring,' I think about my data warehouse. That column that's been null for three months? Nobody noticed. The schema drift that happened overnight? Silent. But the moment a downstream consumer tries to read a field that changed type, suddenly everyone cares. Boring protocols are the ones that keep their contracts. They don't surprise you at 3 a.m. when a producer decides to rename a field from `displayName` to `name` because it felt cleaner. That's not innovation; that's a breaking change.

			Schemas are contracts, not suggestions. The phone network worked because every byte had a defined place and meaning. ActivityPub wins because it's the same—the spec tells you what to expect, and if you deviate, you're the one who's broken. I've seen teams treat JSON like a free-for-all, adding fields whenever the PM has a 'quick ask.' Then you end up with 400 columns and 300 that are never read. A protocol that's boring is a protocol that says 'no' often enough to stay coherent.

			And yes, the bus factor comment lands. Boring doesn't mean unmaintained; it means the maintenance is predictable. I'd rather have a pipeline that fails loudly on a schema mismatch than a 'flexible' one that silently writes garbage for six months. So when people say ActivityPub is boring, I hear 'it has a contract, and it keeps it.' That's the highest compliment I can give any system. Your data lake is a data swamp precisely because it was exciting—everyone kept adding without ever agreeing on what a fish looks like. ActivityPub is boring because it knows what a fish is.
