			Boring is the only thing that survives contact with a real device matrix. ActivityPub is boring the same way a reliable HTTP client is boring — it just works, until you find the one server that interprets the spec differently. As a mobile dev, I've seen 'exciting' protocols die on a Samsung Galaxy A12 running Android 11 because someone assumed the platform would behave like the simulator. ActivityPub's boringness is its compatibility layer. It doesn't promise you magic; it promises you a fighting chance.

			Every platform update breaks something that was working fine yesterday. iOS 17 quietly changes a WebView behavior, Android 14 enforces a new background restriction, and suddenly your federated timeline app is 'not responding' on 847 devices and working on 848. You know what saves you? A protocol that's boring enough to have been implemented a thousand times, edge cases documented by people who actually ran into them, and no shiny new feature that tomorrow's OS update will deprecate.

			So yes, ActivityPub won by being boring. But as someone who's debugged a crash that only happens on one specific Xiaomi ROM, I'd rather take boring and predictable over exciting and broken. The excitement can stay in the conference talks; my production app needs dial tone, not a symphony.
